# Everything here does two jobs: it keeps the build context small, and - the part
# that actually costs minutes - it stops an edit to a file the build never reads
# from invalidating `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile's build stage. That COPY is the
# cache boundary: bust it and `next build` recompiles from cold, so editing the
# README or the Caddyfile used to buy you a full rebuild for nothing.
#
# Anything listed below is therefore either (a) huge, or (b) inert at build time.
# `scripts/` is deliberately NOT here: tsconfig's `include` is `**/*.ts`, so
# `next build` type-checks it, and hiding it would make the Docker build check
# less than the local one does.

# Huge, and rebuilt inside the image anyway.
node_modules
.next
.git

# Runtime state. These are Docker named volumes in production (see
# docker-compose.yml); the copies in the source tree are stale local leftovers and
# must never be baked into an image.
uploads
private-uploads

# Docs. Read by humans, not by `next build`.
*.md

# Loose design assets. Nothing in src/ or the Caddyfile resolves this path -
# images the app actually serves live in public/ or in the uploads volume.
portal

# Secrets. Compose injects the real values as environment at run time, so a build
# that could see .env would only be able to bake them into the image - which is
# precisely what we don't want.
.env
.env.*

# Infrastructure config. Interpreted by Docker and by Caddy - Caddyfile is
# bind-mounted into the caddy container at run time, never copied into this image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
Caddyfile

# Local tool state.
.gitignore
.claude
.vscode
.idea
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log
